{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01PCqVcxcenaCLcCepfQC6uR","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Perfect! Now let me create a comprehensive summary of my findings.\n\n## Summary: SIGPIPE Testing in go-unix-utils\n\nI've completed a thorough search of the codebase for SIGPIPE testing. Here are my findings:\n\n### 1. DiffTest Struct Definition\n\nLocated in `/private/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/5611/pkg/testutils/difftest.go` (lines 46-55):\n\n```go\ntype DiffTest struct {\n\tName          string\n\tArgs          []string\n\tStdin         []byte\n\tEnv           []string\n\tWorkDir       string\n\tExitCode      int\n\tNormalize     []NormalizeFunc\n\tExpectedFiles map[string][]byte\n}\n```\n\n**Field documentation:**\n- **Name**: Identifies the subtest (used with t.Run)\n- **Args**: Command-line arguments passed to both binaries\n- **Stdin**: nil means EOF immediately; empty []byte{} is an open stdin immediately closed\n- **Env**: nil means inherit test process environment with LC_ALL=C; non-nil KEY=VALUE pairs are merged\n- **WorkDir**: Working directory for command execution\n- **ExitCode**: Defaults to 0 when zero value\n- **Normalize**: Slice of NormalizeFunc applied in order to stdout/stderr before comparison\n- **ExpectedFiles**: Map of file paths to expected contents for post-execution file verification\n\nNote: The DiffTest struct does NOT have explicit pipe/stdin handling fields—pipe behavior is controlled entirely through the **Stdin** field and the actual test context (using exec.StdoutPipe() is external to DiffTest).\n\n### 2. SIGPIPE Test Examples Found\n\nOver **25 test files** contain TestSIGPIPE functions. These are NOT differential tests using DiffTest; they are custom integration tests. Here are representative patterns:\n\n#### Pattern A: Simple SIGPIPE Test (e.g., cat, md5sum)\nFrom `/cmd/cat/cat_test.go` lines 83-112 and `/cmd/md5sum/md5sum_test.go` lines 274-303:\n\n```go\nfunc TestSIGPIPE(t *testing.T) {\n\tgoBin := testutils.BuildBinary(t, \".\")\n\tdir := t.TempDir()\n\tlargePath := filepath.Join(dir, \"large.dat\")\n\tif err := os.WriteFile(largePath, bytes.Repeat([]byte(\"x\\n\"), 500000), 0o644); err != nil {\n\t\tt.Fatal(err)\n\t}\n\tctx, cancel := context.WithTimeout(context.Background(), 5*time.Second)\n\tdefer cancel()\n\tcmd := exec.CommandContext(ctx, goBin, largePath)\n\tstdout, err := cmd.StdoutPipe()\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\tt.Fatal(err)\n\t}\n\tif err := cmd.Start(); err != nil {\n\t\tt.Fatal(err)\n\t}\n\tbuf := make([]byte, 1)\n\tif _, err := io.ReadFull(stdout, buf); err != nil {\n\t\tt.Fatal(err)\n\t}\n\tstdout.Close()\n\terr = cmd.Wait()\n\tif ctx.Err() == context.DeadlineExceeded {\n\t\tt.Fatal(\"cat timed out; SIGPIPE handler may not be installed\")\n\t}\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\tt.Fatalf(\"expected exit 0 on SIGPIPE, got: %v\", err)\n\t}\n}\n```\n\n#### Pattern B: Refactored Helper Function (e.g., base32, b2sum)\nFrom `/cmd/b2sum/b2sum_test.go` lines 302-339:\n\n```go\nfunc TestSIGPIPE(t *testing.T) {\n\tgoBin := testutils.BuildBinary(t, \".\")\n\tdir := t.TempDir()\n\tlargePath := filepath.Join(dir, \"large.dat\")\n\tif err := os.WriteFile(largePath, bytes.Repeat([]byte(\"x\\n\"), 500000), 0o644); err != nil {\n\t\tt.Fatal(err)\n\t}\n\tt.Run(\"go\", func(t *testing.T) { testSIGPIPE(t, goBin, largePath) })\n\tif refBin, err := exec.LookPath(\"gb2sum\"); err == nil {\n\t\tt.Run(\"ref\", func(t *testing.T) { testSIGPIPE(t, refBin, largePath) })\n\t}\n}\n\nfunc testSIGPIPE(t *testing.T, bin, filePath string) {\n\tt.Helper()\n\tctx, cancel := context.WithTimeout(context.Background(), 5*time.Second)\n\tdefer cancel()\n\tcmd := exec.CommandContext(ctx, bin, filePath)\n\tstdout, err := cmd.StdoutPipe()\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\tt.Fatal(err)\n\t}\n\tif err := cmd.Start(); err != nil {\n\t\tt.Fatal(err)\n\t}\n\tbuf := make([]byte, 1)\n\tif _, err := io.ReadFull(stdout, buf); err != nil {\n\t\tt.Fatal(err)\n\t}\n\tstdout.Close()\n\terr = cmd.Wait()\n\tif ctx.Err() == context.DeadlineExceeded {\n\t\tt.Fatalf(\"%s timed out; SIGPIPE handler may not be installed\", bin)\n\t}\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\tt.Fatalf(\"%s: expected exit 0 on SIGPIPE, got: %v\", bin, err)\n\t}\n}\n```\n\n#### Pattern C: yes Command Test (Different approach)\nFrom `/cmd/yes/yes_test.go` lines 51-108:\n\n```go\nfunc TestExitCodeOnSIGPIPE(t *testing.T) {\n\tt.Parallel()\n\tgoBin := testutils.BuildBinary(t, \".\")\n\n\tcases := []struct {\n\t\tname string\n\t\targs []string\n\t}{\n\t\t{\"default\", nil},\n\t\t{\"with-arg\", []string{\"hello\"}},\n\t}\n\n\tfor _, tc := range cases {\n\t\tt.Run(tc.name, func(t *testing.T) {\n\t\t\tt.Parallel()\n\t\t\tgoCode, goStderr := captureExitAndStderr(t, goBin, tc.args)\n\t\t\tif goCode != 0 {\n\t\t\t\tt.Errorf(\"expected exit code 0 on broken pipe, got %d\", goCode)\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tif len(goStderr) > 0 {\n\t\t\t\tt.Errorf(\"unexpected stderr on SIGPIPE: %q\", goStderr)\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t})\n\t}\n}\n\nfunc captureExitAndStderr(t *testing.T, binary string, args []string) (int, []byte) {\n\tt.Helper()\n\tctx, cancel := context.WithTimeout(context.Background(), 5*time.Second)\n\tdefer cancel()\n\n\tcmd := exec.CommandContext(ctx, binary, args...)\n\tstdout, err := cmd.StdoutPipe()\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\tt.Fatalf(\"StdoutPipe: %v\", err)\n\t}\n\tvar stderrBuf bytes.Buffer\n\tcmd.Stderr = &stderrBuf\n\tif err := cmd.Start(); err != nil {\n\t\tt.Fatalf(\"Start: %v\", err)\n\t}\n\n\tscanner := bufio.NewScanner(stdout)\n\tif scanner.Scan() {\n\t\t_ = scanner.Text()\n\t}\n\tstdout.Close()\n\n\terr = cmd.Wait()\n\tif err == nil {\n\t\treturn 0, stderrBuf.Bytes()\n\t}\n\tif exitErr, ok := errors.AsType[*exec.ExitError](err); ok {\n\t\treturn exitErr.ExitCode(), stderrBuf.Bytes()\n\t}\n\tt.Fatalf(\"unexpected wait error: %v\", err)\n\treturn -1, nil\n}\n```\n\n### 3. Commands with TestSIGPIPE Tests (25+ found)\n\nFiles with SIGPIPE tests: arch, b2sum, base32, basenc, cat, cksum, comm, du, md5sum, mkfifo, mknod, nice, nl, nohup, pathchk, sha1sum, sha224sum, sha256sum, sha384sum, sha512sum, sum, tac, tsort, unexpand, uniq, yes\n\n### 4. SIGPIPE Handler Implementation\n\nLocated in `/private/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/5611/pkg/sys/sigpipe.go`:\n\n```go\nfunc InstallSIGPIPEHandler() {\n\tc := make(chan os.Signal, 1)\n\tsignal.Notify(c, syscall.SIGPIPE)\n\tgo func() {\n\t\t<-c\n\t\tos.Exit(0)\n\t}()\n}\n```\n\nThis is called at the start of main() in affected commands and ensures exit 0 when stdout/stderr is closed by a downstream consumer (matching GNU coreutils behavior).\n\n### 5. Helper Functions in pkg/testutils/\n\nLocated in `/private/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/5611/pkg/testutils/`:\n\n- **BuildBinary()**: Compiles Go package and returns binary path (in build.go)\n- **RunDiffTests()**: Executes differential tests comparing Go and reference binaries (in difftest.go)\n- **ComposeNormalizers()**: Combines multiple NormalizeFunc functions (in difftest.go)\n- **TimestampNormalizer**: Built-in normalizer for timestamp patterns (in difftest.go)\n\nNote: The DiffTest struct is used for **differential testing** (comparing Go vs GNU implementations), NOT for SIGPIPE-specific testing. SIGPIPE tests are custom integration tests that use exec.StdoutPipe() directly to simulate the broken pipe condition."}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c7ba62e5-5152-4b58-8bb2-97c322bbe1e7","uuid":"7bf5f644-c765-4f6b-957d-feed3b214e53","timestamp":"2026-05-27T20:35:28.127Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"Search the codebase for how SIGPIPE is tested in differential tests. Look for:\n1. Any test files in cmd/ that test SIGPIPE handling (grep for \"SIGPIPE\", \"sigpipe\", \"head\", \"pipe\" in test files)\n2. The DiffTest struct definition in pkg/testutils/difftest.go - I need to see all fields including any pipe/stdin related ones\n3. Any helper functions in pkg/testutils/ related to SIGPIPE testing\n\nReport the full DiffTest struct definition, any SIGPIPE test examples found, and any relevant helper functions. Be thorough.","agentId":"a35155550c6448be1","agentType":"Explore","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Perfect! Now let me create a comprehensive summary of my findings.\n\n## Summary: SIGPIPE Testing in go-unix-utils\n\nI've completed a thorough search of the codebase for SIGPIPE testing. Here are my findings:\n\n### 1. DiffTest Struct Definition\n\nLocated in `/private/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/5611/pkg/testutils/difftest.go` (lines 46-55):\n\n```go\ntype DiffTest struct {\n\tName          string\n\tArgs          []string\n\tStdin         []byte\n\tEnv           []string\n\tWorkDir       string\n\tExitCode      int\n\tNormalize     []NormalizeFunc\n\tExpectedFiles map[string][]byte\n}\n```\n\n**Field documentation:**\n- **Name**: Identifies the subtest (used with t.Run)\n- **Args**: Command-line arguments passed to both binaries\n- **Stdin**: nil means EOF immediately; empty []byte{} is an open stdin immediately closed\n- **Env**: nil means inherit test process environment with LC_ALL=C; non-nil KEY=VALUE pairs are merged\n- **WorkDir**: Working directory for command execution\n- **ExitCode**: Defaults to 0 when zero value\n- **Normalize**: Slice of NormalizeFunc applied in order to stdout/stderr before comparison\n- **ExpectedFiles**: Map of file paths to expected contents for post-execution file verification\n\nNote: The DiffTest struct does NOT have explicit pipe/stdin handling fields—pipe behavior is controlled entirely through the **Stdin** field and the actual test context (using exec.StdoutPipe() is external to DiffTest).\n\n### 2. SIGPIPE Test Examples Found\n\nOver **25 test files** contain TestSIGPIPE functions. These are NOT differential tests using DiffTest; they are custom integration tests. Here are representative patterns:\n\n#### Pattern 